WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:02.670
ELIAS PINTO: Whoa,
it's dark in here.

00:00:02.670 --> 00:00:04.980
JASON BISSON: I'm glad we
have these cave lights.

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:07.320
Did you charge yours?

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:09.570
ELIAS PINTO: Oh, of course not.

00:00:09.570 --> 00:00:11.850
Geez, I guess we're going
to be partially in the dark

00:00:11.850 --> 00:00:12.600
here today.

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:14.160
JASON BISSON: You know who's
personally in the dark?

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:15.960
Some of our cloud
customers out there,

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:18.150
with multiple logging platforms.

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:21.990
And they're busy searching for
who did what, where, and when.

00:00:21.990 --> 00:00:23.880
The great news is,
at Google Cloud,

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:26.220
we have a solution that will
help our customers get out

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:28.410
of the dark by
exporting those audit

00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:31.410
events into a popular logging
platform called Splunk.

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:32.460
You want to check it out?

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:33.501
ELIAS PINTO: Let's do it.

00:00:33.501 --> 00:00:37.245
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:39.334 --> 00:00:41.750
JASON BISSON: So the first
thing we're going to need to do

00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:43.620
is create a topic.

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:46.910
In this case, we're just
going to name it gcp2splunk.

00:00:46.910 --> 00:00:49.320
We're going to click
on the Create button.

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:52.850
And the topic is going to
be created quite quickly.

00:00:52.850 --> 00:00:56.150
The next step is to
create the export sync.

00:00:56.150 --> 00:01:00.170
To do this, we're going
to open up a Cloud Shell.

00:01:00.170 --> 00:01:03.650
And then we're going to download
a script from a Google Cloud

00:01:03.650 --> 00:01:06.080
Storage bucket that has
the G Cloud commands

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:08.450
from our solution guide.

00:01:08.450 --> 00:01:10.640
Now that the script
is downloaded,

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:13.010
we're going to pass
it the argument

00:01:13.010 --> 00:01:14.930
of the organizational name.

00:01:14.930 --> 00:01:17.570
And that's important
to note because when

00:01:17.570 --> 00:01:19.460
we run that command
with the filter,

00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:21.440
it will include the
children records.

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:24.230
And that will include the
folders and projects--

00:01:24.230 --> 00:01:27.410
any events underneath the org.

00:01:27.410 --> 00:01:30.542
Now you'll notice that from
those G Cloud commands,

00:01:30.542 --> 00:01:32.000
there's a couple
of messages there.

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:34.250
And one is indicating
a new service

00:01:34.250 --> 00:01:36.590
account has been created.

00:01:36.590 --> 00:01:38.390
And it's asking for
that service account

00:01:38.390 --> 00:01:42.400
to have the Pub/Sub
publisher role.

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:44.690
The reason that it needs
a Pub/Sub publisher

00:01:44.690 --> 00:01:47.420
role is because those
exported events from the sync

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:49.770
are going to go to the topic.

00:01:49.770 --> 00:01:52.480
So now we'll go back
to the Pub/Sub topic.

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:57.260
We'll click on Permissions, and
we'll paste in the new service

00:01:57.260 --> 00:01:58.160
account.

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:01.820
We're going to need to give it
that Pub/Sub publisher role,

00:02:01.820 --> 00:02:04.730
and we'll click on Add.

00:02:04.730 --> 00:02:06.350
So now we have a topic.

00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:08.240
We have an export sync.

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:12.330
The next step that we'll need
to do is create a subscription.

00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:14.780
And this is a
subscription that Splunk

00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:17.970
will have to pull those
messages from the topic.

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:20.580
So we'll click on
New Subscription.

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:22.250
We're going to
name it splunk2gcp.

00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:28.700
The delivery type
is going to be Pull.

00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:30.140
And then we'll click on Create.

00:02:32.860 --> 00:02:35.280
And just like the topic,
that happens pretty quickly.

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:37.070
So now we have a
subscription for Splunk

00:02:37.070 --> 00:02:39.904
to pull the messages.

00:02:39.904 --> 00:02:41.570
The next thing we're
going to need to do

00:02:41.570 --> 00:02:44.360
is we're going to have
to give Splunk permission

00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:47.750
to come into the GCP platform
and pull the messages.

00:02:47.750 --> 00:02:49.450
We'll go up to the IAM roles.

00:02:49.450 --> 00:02:51.230
We'll click on Service Accounts.

00:02:51.230 --> 00:02:54.980
Then we'll click on
Create Service Account.

00:02:54.980 --> 00:02:57.320
And here, we're going
to name it splunk2gcp.

00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:02.150
And we're going to give it
a couple of different roles

00:03:02.150 --> 00:03:03.540
that are required.

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:07.380
One is going to be the
project viewer role.

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:11.395
The second role will be, of
course, the Pub/Sub subscriber

00:03:11.395 --> 00:03:12.990
role.

00:03:12.990 --> 00:03:16.970
So now we have two roles, a
viewer, and Pub/Sub subscriber.

00:03:16.970 --> 00:03:19.190
We're going to click on
Furnish a New Private

00:03:19.190 --> 00:03:25.122
Key, with a key type of
JSON, and click Create.

00:03:25.122 --> 00:03:26.580
You'll notice on
the left-hand side

00:03:26.580 --> 00:03:30.060
that that JSON key
has been downloaded.

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:31.860
And we have a couple
of warnings there

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:34.170
about protecting that key.

00:03:34.170 --> 00:03:36.570
And it's very important
that you consider

00:03:36.570 --> 00:03:39.030
integrating this process
into your secrets management

00:03:39.030 --> 00:03:43.290
platform, because this will
give access to the GCP platform.

00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:47.040
And also shred any local
copies that you have there.

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:50.070
So now that we understand that
we have to secure this key,

00:03:50.070 --> 00:03:52.710
we'll click Close.

00:03:52.710 --> 00:03:57.310
And we'll head back to the topic
and try to publish a message.

00:03:57.310 --> 00:04:02.485
We'll click on Topics, and we'll
click on Publish a Message.

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:12.680
And we'll type in, "Help,
I'm in a cave with clowns,"

00:04:12.680 --> 00:04:14.450
because that's not scary.

00:04:14.450 --> 00:04:17.240
And we'll click on Publish.

00:04:17.240 --> 00:04:19.160
The next thing we could
do is we can leverage

00:04:19.160 --> 00:04:22.190
the metrics to explore in
Stackdriver to check out

00:04:22.190 --> 00:04:24.530
that messages are being
published successfully

00:04:24.530 --> 00:04:27.030
and can be used for
troubleshooting in the future.

00:04:27.030 --> 00:04:27.930
So that's it.

00:04:27.930 --> 00:04:30.680
We now have audit events from
the Google Cloud Platform

00:04:30.680 --> 00:04:33.030
ready to be pulled into
your Splunk instance,

00:04:33.030 --> 00:04:36.140
making it easier to answer
the question of who did what,

00:04:36.140 --> 00:04:37.190
where, and when.

00:04:37.190 --> 00:04:39.770
ELIAS PINTO: So this does make
it easy for security ops teams

00:04:39.770 --> 00:04:41.609
to have one place to
look for audit events.

00:04:41.609 --> 00:04:42.650
JASON BISSON: You got it.

00:04:42.650 --> 00:04:45.570
ELIAS PINTO: Don't forget to
like, comment, and subscribe.

00:04:45.570 --> 00:04:47.780
And if you're working
on any cool projects

00:04:47.780 --> 00:04:49.820
and would like to get
in contact with us,

00:04:49.820 --> 00:04:51.867
click on the link
in the description.

00:04:51.867 --> 00:04:52.700
Thanks for watching.

00:04:52.700 --> 00:04:56.350
[MUSIC PLAYING]

